Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Emergency Medicine Physician (Lead)): The lead clinician who performs the initial evaluation, stabilizes the patient, and initiates immediate management, including crucial interventions for chest pain.
 Member 2 (Cardiologist): Provides specialized input regarding the potential cardiac implications of the patient's symptoms and can assist in managing acute coronary syndromes.
 Member 3 (Critical Care Nurse): Supports the assessment and triage of the patient, monitoring vital signs and implementing initial care protocols to ensure patient stability.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Radiologist (Lead)): Responsible for interpreting any diagnostic imaging studies, such as an electrocardiogram (ECG) and chest X
 Member 2 (Laboratory Technician): Conducts and processes necessary lab tests, including cardiac biomarkers, electrolytes, and complete blood count (CBC), providing essential information for diagnosis.
 Member 3 (Pharmacist): Evaluates current medications and assesses any drug interactions or contraindications concerning management options available for this patient, especially given his cocaine use.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Consultant Cardiologist (Lead)): Provides expertise to finalize the assessment results and guides the management plan based on cardiovascular health and risk factors.
 Member 2 (Emergency Medicine Consultant): Facilitates the decision
 Member 3 (Clinical Decision Support Specialist): Coordinates the recommendations from both the Initial Assessment and Diagnostic Evidence Teams to formulate a comprehensive and effective management strategy tailored to the patient’s condition.


