[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Pulmonologist (Lead)): Specializes in respiratory conditions and will assess the patient's pulmonary status, interpret imaging findings, and guide initial management of the suspected pneumonia.
 Member 2 (Infectious Disease Specialist): Provides expertise in identifying the likely pathogens causing the infection and recommends appropriate empirical antibiotic therapy based on the patient's clinical presentation and risk factors.
 Member 3 (Hospitalist or General Internist): Oversees the patient's overall medical management during hospitalization, coordinating care among various specialties and evaluating for other potential causes of fever and cough.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Radiologist (Lead)): Reviews chest radiographs and any advanced imaging to confirm the presence and extent of consolidation, helping to differentiate between possible infections and other pulmonary conditions.
 Member 2 (Microbiologist): Responsible for interpreting culture results from sputum or blood, guiding the choice of antimicrobials based on laboratory findings and local resistance patterns.
 Member 3 (Nurse Practitioner (Infectious Disease)): Assists with evaluating laboratory results, tracking patient progress, and implementing infection control measures while liaising between the physician team and nursing staff.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Pulmonologist (Lead)): Provides final expert input on the diagnosis and management strategies for the patient's respiratory condition, ensuring all aspects of care are considered in the treatment plan.
 Member 2 (Clinical Pharmacologist): Evaluates antibiotic choices and their potential interactions with the patient’s other medications, ensuring safe and effective therapy is implemented and monitored.
 Member 3 (Geriatrician): Considers the patient's age and comorbidities in the context of treatment options, providing insights into tailored management plans that accommodate the patient's unique health profile and recovery potential.


